{"text": "L\n\nStudy Group Program\n(September 12, 1986)\n\nTotal Available Finds = $353k\n\nA.\n\n968k Quality Assurance of Experinental Cigarettes\n\nAfter considerable solicitation, CFR has determined that the\nquality assurance measurements and analysis of the experinental\ncigarettes will be significantly less costly than anticipated.\n\u2018This sum covers the in-house effort and subcontracting to obtain\nthe data and the statistical treatment of the results.\n\n$10k California BHF Test Data\n\n\u2018The California Bureau of Home Furnishings {s generating mock-up\nand! full-scale data on furniture ignition by the standard test\ncigarette. A small grant would enable them to prepare an interim\nreport (>400 items) comparing the two sets of results, thus\nhelping us determine the degree to vhich the small scale results\ncan be used to predict real-life ignicability.\n\n915k Cigarette Performance Data\n\nCurrent research at NBS has identified sone traits of the\nexperimental cigarettes that correlate with reduced ignition\nPropensity. This project vould involve the confirmation of those\nresults at an independent lab, such as the California Bureau of\nHome Furnishings.\n\n$65k Alkal{ Metall Ion Effects\n\nTt has long been known that compounds containing alkali metal ions\nProuote the smoldering of fabrics and padding. The concentration\nof such compounds is not controlled im the manufacturing process\nand may account for the poor reproducibility of laboratory\nresults. Furthermore, such compounds could be deposited on\nfurniture fabrics during use, especially from people sweating. To\nPredict the reduction in cigarette {gnitions using laboratory test\nresults, the effect of the alkali ion concentrations in the test\nmaterials must be quantified.\n\nSE6TTSz00T\n\n48",
